@FX P_21CARD|THE 21 CARD DEAL|performer|teach_21
@GAP which column they point to, three times|their card|Performer mode: the device is your accomplice, not the mind reader. The spectator never tells YOU the card - only a column - and three columns cannot name one of 21 cards except through the deal's structure.
@P idle
@S A self-working classic. No skill needed.
@W 800
@B press to learn it
@P think
@S Deal 21 cards face up: 3 columns of 7.
@W 900
@S Ask them to think of one card. Don't touch it.
@W 900
@B next
@S Ask which column it's in.
@W 700
@S Gather the columns - THEIRS GOES IN THE MIDDLE.
@W 1000
@B next
@S Re-deal 3 columns of 7. Ask again. Middle again.
@W 900
@S Do it a third time.
@B next
@P raise
@S Their card is now the 11th you deal.
@W 900
@S Count to eleven and turn it over.
@W 800
@P reveal
@R ALWAYS THE ELEVENTH CARD
@W 600
@S The deal does the work. You just count.
@E
@FX P_GILBREATH|THE HONEST SHUFFLE|performer|teach_gilbreath
@GAP nothing from the spectator|that every pair splits one red one black|Performer mode. The spectator shuffles genuinely; the Gilbreath principle guarantees the pairing regardless, so there is nothing to learn and nothing to fake.
@P idle
@S They shuffle. It still works. Really.
@W 800
@B press to learn it
@P think
@S Set up: alternate red, black, red, black.
@W 900
@S All 52, strictly alternating.
@B next
@S Hand it over. Let them cut it anywhere.
@W 800
@S Have them deal some cards to the table.
@W 800
@S That reverses that packet - it's the secret.
@B next
@S Now let them riffle the two piles together.
@W 900
@S A genuine, messy shuffle. Their hands.
@B next
@P raise
@S Deal the deck in PAIRS.
@W 800
@P reveal
@R EVERY PAIR: ONE RED, ONE BLACK
@W 700
@S Twenty-six times running. It cannot fail.
@E
@FX P_NIM|THE MATCH GAME|performer|teach_nim
@GAP how many they take each turn|that you always take the last one|Performer mode. It is a strategy, openly stated: keep each round to five and you cannot lose. Nothing is hidden from the device and nothing needs to be.
@P idle
@S A bar bet you will never lose.
@W 800
@B press to learn it
@P think
@S Put out 20 matches. Or coins. Anything.
@W 900
@S Each turn, a player takes 1, 2, 3 or 4.
@W 800
@S Whoever takes the last one wins.
@B next
@S Let THEM go first. Always.
@W 800
@P raise
@S Then make every round total five.
@W 900
@S They take 1, you take 4. They take 3, you take 2.
@W 1000
@B next
@P reveal
@R KEEP EACH ROUND AT FIVE AND THE LAST IS YOURS
@W 700
@S Twenty is four fives. The maths is doing it.
@E
@FX P_1089|THE SEALED TOTAL|performer|teach_1089
@GAP nothing from the spectator|a total written before they started|Performer mode. You write 1089 in advance; every legal start converges there, so the spectator's choices are free and the prediction still matches.
@P idle
@S Write a prediction. Fold it. Hand it over.
@W 900
@B press to learn it
@P think
@S Write 1089 on the paper. Seal it.
@W 900
@S Now: any three-digit number, they choose.
@W 800
@S First digit two or more bigger than the last.
@B next
@S Reverse. Subtract smaller from larger.
@W 800
@S Reverse that answer. Add the two.
@B next
@P raise
@S Have them open your prediction.
@W 900
@P reveal
@R 1089 - EVERY TIME
@W 700
@S You never saw a number they picked.
@E
@FX P_SQUARE|THE SUM SQUARE|performer|teach_square
@GAP the target sum they name|a square where every line hits their target|Performer mode. They name the target aloud, so the device knowing it is not a leak - the astonishment is the construction, not secret knowledge.
@P idle
@S They pick a total. You build the square.
@W 900
@B press to learn it
@P think
@S Memorise this grid: 8 1 6 / 3 5 7 / 4 9 2.
@W 1100
@S Every line sums to fifteen.
@B next
@S Ask for a total divisible by three.
@W 800
@S Subtract fifteen. Divide by three.
@W 800
@S Add THAT to all nine cells.
@B next
@P raise
@S Example: they say 30.
@W 700
@S 30-15=15, over 3 is 5. Add 5 to each cell.
@W 1000
@S 13 6 11 / 8 10 12 / 9 14 7.
@B next
@P reveal
@R EVERY ROW COLUMN AND DIAGONAL HITS THEIR NUMBER
@W 700
@S Nine sums, all correct, built in your head.
@E
@FX P_KRUSKAL|THE CONVERGING PATH|performer|teach_kruskal
@GAP nothing|that two freely chosen paths meet|Performer mode, and honestly framed: this one is about eighty-five percent, not certain. The device says so, and teaches the out for when it misses.
@P idle
@S This one is NOT certain. About 85 percent.
@W 1000
@S So you learn the out too.
@B press to learn it
@P think
@S Deal the deck face up in a long row.
@W 900
@S They pick a secret start: any of the first ten.
@W 900
@B next
@S Count that many along. Land on a card.
@W 800
@S Its value is the next count. Repeat to the end.
@W 1000
@B next
@S You do the same from card ONE. Silently.
@W 900
@P raise
@S Your chains almost always collide.
@W 900
@B next
@P reveal
@R NAME THE CARD YOUR OWN CHAIN ENDED ON
@W 800
@S If it misses: 'your path resisted me' and deal again.
@E
@FX P_CARD|NAME THEIR CARD|performer|card_10v_s
@GAP one total, said aloud|the card they are holding|Performer mode: the spectator says a total; (v*2+3)*5+s-15 is 10v+s, decoded on a screen they cannot see.
@P idle
@S They pick any card and hide it.
@B next
@S SAY: Ace is 1, Jack 11, Queen 12, King 13.
@B next
@S SAY: Double it. Add three. Times five.
@B next
@S SAY: Add 1 clubs, 2 hearts, 3 spades, 4 diamonds. Your total?
@B next
@A type the total they said|26|149|0
@X 1|s0 15 - 10 %
@X 0|s0 15 - 10 /
@P reveal
@R {CARD s0 s1}
@W 600
@S Pause. Stare. Then name it slowly.
@E
@FX P_BDAY|NAME THEIR BIRTHDAY|performer|bday_100m_d
@GAP one total, said aloud|their birthday|Performer mode: ((((m*5+6)*4+9)*5+d)-165) is 100m+d.
@P idle
@S SAY: Birth month as a number. Times five.
@B next
@S SAY: Add six. Times four. Add nine.
@B next
@S SAY: Times five. Add the day you were born.
@B next
@S SAY: Subtract 165. What do you get?
@B next
@A type the total they said|101|1231|0
@X 1|s0 100 %
@X 0|s0 100 /
@P reveal
@R {MONTH s0} {s1}
@W 600
@S Say the month first. Let the day land.
@E
@FX P_CLOCK|NAME THEIR TIME|performer|clock_100h_m
@GAP one total, said aloud|the time they pictured|Performer mode: ((h*2+5)*50+m)-250 is 100h+m.
@P idle
@S SAY: Picture a clock. Any hour, any minute.
@B next
@S SAY: Hour times two. Add five. Times fifty.
@B next
@S SAY: Add the minutes. Subtract 250. Total?
@B next
@A type the total they said|100|1259|0
@X 1|s0 100 %
@X 0|s0 100 /
@P reveal
@R {CLOCK s0 s1}
@W 600
@S Tell them the hands. Then the minutes.
@E
@FX P_FIB|LIGHTNING SUM|performer|fib_eleven
@GAP nothing|how to add ten numbers instantly|Performer mode: for any seeds, the sum of the first ten terms is exactly 11 x the 7th term.
@P idle
@S They write two numbers, one under the other.
@B next
@S Each new number = the two above added.
@B next
@S Ten numbers. Then you glance once.
@B next
@P think
@S Take the SEVENTH number. Times eleven.
@B next
@S Times 11: write it, then add it shifted one place.
@B next
@P reveal
@R TOTAL = 7TH x 11
@W 700
@S Write your total down BEFORE they finish adding.
@E
